    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2096200723671446017",
      "url": "https://x.com/Trader_XO/status/2096200723671446017",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/Trader_XO/status/2096200723671446017",
      "text": "The trenches are fun, no doubt, and there\u2019s nothing wrong with exploring them. New markets, new structures and new opportunities are part of trading... staying curious and open minded matters. \n\nThe danger is when curiosity turns into FOMO and a hyper gambling state of mind, constantly chasing the next ticker, the next runner, the next 10x because your timeline makes it look like the easiest money in the market.\n\nTo consistently make money in the trenches, you probably need some combination of sophisticated systems/software that identify opportunities early, strong position sizing and trade management, the ability to recognise genuine winners and size into them appropriately, or some form of informational advantage that the average participant simply doesn\u2019t have. \n\nThe other side of that equation is being part of the game itself... launching tokens, promoting them, controlling distribution, or having access to flows and information that others don\u2019t.\n\nAnd realistically, not everything is always what it appears to be. The statistics coming out are hardly surprising... the majority don\u2019t make much, while a relatively small percentage capture most of the upside. \n\nSocial media naturally amplifies the winners, the huge multiples and the overnight success stories, while behind the scenes there can be , teams, insiders, KOL allocations, side wallets, coordinated flows and plenty of questionable practices. \n\nBut that\u2019s part of understanding the game rather than complaining about it. You either understand the environment, develop an edge within it and participate accordingly... or you don\u2019t.\n\nThe important part is knowing what you\u2019re actually good at. If your edge is in the trenches, then become exceptional at it... build the systems, understand the flows, learn the participants and specialise in that environment. \n\nPersonally, my edge has always been spot and futures across crypto and global markets... understanding price, positioning, context, risk and recognising when conditions are favourable enough to press. \n\nThat doesn\u2019t mean ignoring new opportunities. Explore them, trade them small, learn from them and remain adaptable... but don\u2019t abandon a proven process because somebody else appears to be making easier money somewhere else. You cannot borrow somebody else\u2019s edge.\n\nThe long game is knowing where your statistical edge lies, understanding the conditions in which you perform best and having the patience to let that compound over time. It isn\u2019t particularly viral... nobody gets excited about taking singles, managing risk, sitting out poor conditions and compounding gradually. \n\nBut when you study many of the best traders, including plenty of the Market Wizards, the recurring theme is usually the same... they know what they\u2019re good at, they know when their edge is present and they don\u2019t feel obligated to participate in every game the market offers.\n\nKnow your game, know your edge... and be open minded without losing sight of either.\n\nHave a fab weekend!",
